@

Notable deltas from issue II: routes 48 $\to$ 60 (program-source compile
routes, mosaic share, deepzoom management); DSLs 2 $\to$ 4 (root and
solve-score gained full source layers); test files 116 $\to$ 143; the
frontend grew $\sim$2.5k lines while \emph{deleting} chips, WASM, and
workers --- the growth is editors, help generation, and mosaics.

\section{Architecture: verified verdicts}

Sections D1--D9 carry over from issues I--II; each verdict below is the
\emph{re-verified} current state. D10 is new.

\subsection{D1 --- Buildless vanilla JS, 13 ordered parts}

Confirmed as described in issue II, with the split now carrying real
weight: 13 classic-script parts (943--2{,}655 lines) registered in
\texttt{window.\_\_ppParts} with a load-order consistency check, plus 9
generated bundles, all under build-versioned asset keys
(\texttt{assets/<BUILD\_ID>/...}) so a deploy flips atomically. A
lineage correction this issue owes the record: \textbf{PolyPaint's
frontend never contained WASM or Web Workers} --- zero references even
in the pre-split 19{,}989-line monolith. The in-browser solver
(\texttt{step\_loop.c} $\to$ WASM, multi-worker fast mode, 40 WASM
tests) belongs to PolyPaint's \emph{predecessor}, the standalone root
visualizer that still lives beside it in the repository; stale project
notes carried its WASM lore forward until code-review-18 (June 19)
verified none of it applies here. The browser is, and always was, a
thin client: programs compile in Python, execute in C; the page edits
text, posts JSON, and polls.

\verdict{Working as intended. The ESM conversion (stage 2) remains
unstarted and is now \emph{less} urgent: the parts have stabilized, the
harness executes them in-context, and the state surface shrank when chips
died. Still missing --- and still cheap --- is static analysis:
\textbf{no ESLint, no tsc --checkJs, no lint config exists anywhere in
the tree} (verified). That gate would have caught the serializer
recursion of issue I and costs days.}

\subsection{D2 --- Manifest-driven bash deployer}

Confirmed and stable. \texttt{deploy\_manifest.json} (711 lines) carries
41 function specs and 60 routes; \texttt{deploy\_manifest.py} validates
uniqueness, single-owner route ownership, layer whitelists, retired-name
collisions, and the layer-only \texttt{LD\_LIBRARY\_PATH} rule (the D9
incident lesson, now a schema rule). deploy.sh is 1{,}993 lines and emits
its own sources from the manifest. \texttt{show-build} and the
post-deploy INIT sweep close the loop after every deploy.

\verdict{Done; unchanged since issue II except growth (60 routes). The
one refinement worth taking: three generators still run only inside
deploy.sh (tri/long palette catalogs, coeff function catalog) rather than
under the predeploy \texttt{--check} pattern the other six follow --- see
N5.}

\subsection{D3 --- 41 Lambdas, shared code copied per zip}

Confirmed unchanged: exactly three \emph{binary} layers exist (libvips,
LAPACK, pdf-python); there is still \textbf{no shared-code layer}, and
\texttt{shared.py}, \texttt{solve\_score\_chain.py}, palette-name
modules, and sidecar helpers are copied into zips per function (finalize
alone bundles seven helper modules). The mid-deploy version-skew window
is real but has never bitten; the packaging tests and the lean-bundle
discipline (a helper added to a shared module can break ten zips, which
is why \texttt{solve\_score\_chain} deliberately keeps a local
number-format copy, parity-pinned) contain it.

\verdict{Keep the topology. The shared-code layer remains the cheapest
open architecture item, but its payoff shrank as the packaging tests
matured; it is now hygiene, not risk reduction. Do it opportunistically,
not urgently.}

\subsection{D4 --- Python around native C via JSON-over-stdin}

Confirmed, and the ``generate the mirrors'' program is complete well
beyond what issue II recorded. \textbf{Eight generators} now emit every
cross-language mirror: opcodes (\texttt{merged\_opcodes} $\to$ Python + C
header + JS), program profiles ($\to$ Python + JS), four registry
vocabularies (param, coeff, solve-score, root $\to$ JS), the coefficient
function catalog ($\to$ C header + JS), and parity fixtures. The last
hand-written frontend catalog (\texttt{\_rtCatalog} for root transforms)
was deleted on July 3; the root registry gained ui blocks whose
descriptions were written from the C implementations in
\texttt{root\_xforms.h}, not from memory.

\verdict{Done. The remaining hand-synchronized C surface (fn\_index
packing ladders, metric tables, caps) is drift-pinned test-by-test ---
partition tables, min-roots table, root cap pinned three ways
(profile$\,=\,$Python$\,=\,$parsed C \texttt{\#define}). The correct next
step is not more generation but property-based parity (N7): random
programs through Python fold vs C eval.}

\subsection{D5 --- The program DSLs: two became four, and text won}

The biggest divergence between proposal and outcome. Issue II's
alternative 4 --- ``make text the only source and render chips as a
read-only visualization; viable only after the side-by-side editor
exists'' --- was implemented \emph{directly}, skipping the intermediate:
Param and Coeff chips were dropped June 22, Root chips July 3;
solve-score chips survive only as read-only strips inside the saved
program modal. Every kind gained a real source layer: parser with
line/column diagnostics and stable machine codes, compiler, fingerprint,
and an HTTP compile route (\texttt{/compile-\{param, coeff, root,
solve-score\}-program-source}). Root and solve-score joined param/coeff
as first-class program kinds under a shared \texttt{program\_profiles}
registry.

Two properties of the shipped design deserve the record, because they
were not in any proposal:

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.4em,itemsep=1pt]
  \item \textbf{Spelling preservation.} Loading a saved program and
  saving it back is byte- and fingerprint-identical (the original row is
  stashed at hydration and re-emitted verbatim while pristine); any edit
  respells. This closed a whole family of ``load then save churns the
  artifact key'' defects.
  \item \textbf{Wire-preserving validation.} Compile-time additions
  (e.g.\ the static division-by-zero guard) are validators, never
  folders: emitted tokens stay byte-identical for every previously valid
  program. The cache keys make this discipline mandatory; the review
  cycle made it explicit policy.
\end{itemize}

\verdict{Settled, and better than proposed. Four kinds, one editor
pattern, all vocabularies generated, chips retired. The two C program VMs
remain two (plus the root/solve-score evaluators) --- unification is
still correctly queued behind a fingerprint version bump that no feature
yet demands. Residue: the dead chip-editing machinery (add/move/remove,
editable-chip renderers, orphaned CSS) is still in the tree --- see N2.}

\subsection{D6 --- Fingerprint cache and format generations}

Correction to issues I--II, which claimed ``nothing in the key encodes
format generation.'' Verified: both param and coeff fingerprints hash
\texttt{\{"version": 1, "execution\_spec": ...\}} --- a version field
exists \emph{inside} the hashed payload, so a deliberate global
invalidation is a one-line bump. What does not exist is per-artifact
dual-read migration. Separately, program \emph{objects} now carry
\texttt{spec\_version: 2} and \texttt{"v2;"}-prefixed spec strings
(v1$\to$v2 migration routes shipped and lifecycle-tested), so the
program-identity layer already did the migration this section once asked
for.

\verdict{Keep. The freeze is real but the escape hatches exist at both
layers. Write down the bump procedure (when to bump, what re-renders)
rather than building more mechanism.}

\subsection{D7 --- SFN + DynamoDB status + polling}

Confirmed unchanged: the browser's only live signal is still HTTP polling
(3-second \texttt{setInterval} on \texttt{/check-status}); there is no
WebSocket, SSE, or push anywhere; no route surfaces Step Functions
execution history (the only SFN API called anywhere is
\texttt{start\_execution}). Two localStorage records let an active
render/palette run survive a reload --- resume, not visibility.

\verdict{Unchanged, and now the clearest architecture--UI gap in the
system: the backend knows job topology precisely and the frontend shows
appended text lines. The jobs rail (old C, unbuilt) plus a cheap
read-only execution-history route remains the recommendation, in that
order.}

\subsection{D8 --- The verification stack}

Substantially stronger than issue II recorded, and one of its two
``missing legs'' turns out to exist:

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.4em,itemsep=1pt]
  \item \textbf{Gate completeness is now enforced, not hoped for.}
  \texttt{test\_predeploy\_gate\_completeness.py} pins: every
  \texttt{tests/test\_*.py} on disk must be in the predeploy gate or in a
  frozen allow-list --- a new test file that isn't wired in fails CI.
  Issue II's H8 observation (``100 of 140 files ungated'') is not just
  fixed; the failure mode is retired. The gate now runs 72 test files +
  the JS harness: 860 passed, 77 subtests at HEAD.
  \item \textbf{Frozen oracles.} Five \texttt{*\_legacy.py} modules
  (4{,}494 lines) snapshot the param/coeff parsers and compilers; current
  parsers are tested against them on a harvested corpus with pinned
  counts (so ``0 cases checked'' can never pass), under an explicit
  never-edit policy. This is the strongest anti-regression device in the
  tree.
  \item \textbf{Playwright exists.} Seven E2E specs
  (\texttt{tests/e2e/}: compute, results, palette, render,
  render-solve-score, favorites, deepzoom-inventory) with a config and
  pinned \texttt{@playwright/test} --- present since April, invoked
  manually (\texttt{npx playwright test}), \emph{not} wired into any
  gate. Issue II called this leg missing; it is actually built and
  unplugged (N6).
  \item \textbf{Property-based testing} remains genuinely absent (no
  hypothesis anywhere) --- the one leg still missing.
\end{itemize}

\verdict{The standout, now with teeth (meta-gated, oracle-pinned).
Remaining: wire the existing Playwright specs into a gate; add
property-based VM parity.}

\subsection{D9 --- Native library exposure}

Confirmed settled and untouched since issue II: four link modes, the
manifest-level \texttt{LD\_LIBRARY\_PATH} rule, Docker-gate loader
assertions over 14 deploy binaries, layer hygiene pins, and the INIT
sweep (which passed after both July deploys). No new incident.

\verdict{Settled; nothing new to add, which is the point.}

\subsection{D10 --- The adversarial-review method itself (new)}

Between issues II and III the project ran five review cycles culminating
in code-review-23: 37 verified findings, fixed in waves, each wave gated,
committed, and recorded in the review document with commit hashes;
findings closed \emph{by decision} are recorded as accepted debt so they
cannot silently reopen. Two structural inventions came out of it worth
naming as architecture:

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.4em,itemsep=1pt]
  \item \textbf{Auto-classified diagnostics.} Error types upgrade a bare
  default code from the message at construction, so $\sim$90 legacy raise
  sites gained stable machine codes without editing any site --- and the
  oracle comparison permits refining the legacy default while pinning
  specific codes strictly.
  \item \textbf{Schema exemptions as tests.} The one intentional
  runtime/display arity mismatch (\texttt{pow}) lived as an explicit
  allow-listed test exemption until the registry was normalized ---
  after which \emph{deleting the exemption} was the proof of completion.
  Exceptions are pinned artifacts, not lore.
\end{itemize}

\verdict{Keep running the method. Its cost is real (the July 2 batch was
$\sim$20 commits of fixes plus documentation) and its yield --- every
class of drift found before production --- has been consistently worth
it.}

\subsection{Decision matrix}

<<matrix, results='asis'>>=
